---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About the react-websocket Astro demo">
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>The connection survived the navigation</h2>
    <p>
      The name next to the status above didn’t change: the server names every connection, so a
      new socket would have come back with a new name. The layout’s
      <code>&lt;ClientRouter /&gt;</code> swaps pages without a full page load, so the shared client
      stays in memory. The status badge is persisted with <code>transition:persist</code>, so it
      stays mounted and holds the connection while the old page’s islands unmount and the new
      page’s hydrate. Go <a href="/">back to the demo</a> and the cards rejoin the same socket.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without the router, every link is a full page load, and every page load is a new connection.
    </p>

    <h2>How the example is built</h2>
    <p>
      Astro endpoints can’t accept WebSocket upgrades. In development, a small integration in
      <code>astro.config.ts</code> attaches the demo’s WebSocket server to Astro’s dev server. In
      production, <code>server.ts</code> serves the build with Express and handles upgrades on the
      same port.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pages are prerendered to static HTML. Only <code>/api/token</code> runs on demand: it
      issues the short-lived token the WebSocket URL carries, and the client fetches a fresh one
      before every connection attempt.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
